This part owns the always-on observability session, the report files it writes, what the screens
are allowed to see, and what happens on shutdown. CROOKS_TEST_SESSION_ALWAYS=true, so this session
is running in production right now.

Answer these, on the code, naming file and symbol:
  - F-04-STARTUP / F-04-REPORT-LOSS: the start-up pass tightens report paths others can reach and
    withholds what it cannot fix. On this host it already tightened two paths and the count is now
    zero. Judge the GENERAL case: can the pass lose a report, or leave one readable, on a path
    shape this host does not happen to have (a symlink, a path it cannot chmod, a race with a
    writer)?
  - F-04-SHUTDOWN: does the Housekeeper/lifespan stop cleanly, and can a shutdown lose a report or
    a timeline that had been accepted?
  - F-10: re-rule at this SHA.
  - tests/test_screen_privacy.py grows by 674 lines. CROOKS_SCREEN_SNAPSHOTS=false in production.
    What exactly is a screen allowed to observe with that switch false, and does anything in this
    range widen it? A screen is a TV in a shop; anything it can see is effectively public.

